+# Decision: Deliver committed changes over self-hosted WebSockets
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard maintains one authoritative, revisioned board per event. Editors submit changes over HTTPS, while as many as 800 simultaneous staff browsers need to reflect committed changes and recover after connectivity interruptions. Connected browsers must show changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity; lower latency is useful even though subsecond delivery is not a requirement.
+
+The delivery mechanism determines more than the server-to-browser transport. It shapes how the change store supports reads or replay, how delivery publishes changes, and how browsers detect interruption and catch up. Choosing it therefore commits the change-store, delivery, and browser-sync components to a shared architecture. The central trade-off is between operational simplicity, update latency, and independence from a realtime provider.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Update latency — major.** Prefer prompt delivery after a commit, while treating the 15-second freshness goal as a minimum rather than a reason to seek subsecond delivery at any cost.
+- **Connection and recovery operations — major.** Prefer a system the team can operate reliably at 800 simultaneous browsers, including capacity management, interruption handling, and recovery. Operational simplicity matters, but does not automatically outweigh the other criteria.
+- **Vendor independence and recurring cost — major.** Prefer avoiding provider-specific delivery behavior and recurring realtime-service fees where the team can accept the resulting operational work. This applies the HLD tenet that both self-hosted and managed infrastructure are permitted.
+- **Server and browser implementation burden — moderate.** Prefer a small, coherent design across revision storage, publication, and browser synchronization. Complexity is acceptable when it directly supports the chosen latency and ownership trade-off.
+- **Authoritative recovery — moderate.** Prefer a delivery model that can restore the latest authoritative board after an interruption of up to 20 minutes without treating a stale browser view as current. This applies the HLD tenets that each committed edit has one authoritative event revision and that connectivity failures remain visible.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers request changes every five seconds over stateless HTTPS. Each request supplies a cursor identifying the last revision observed. The change store answers cursor queries, and the browser replaces or advances its view from the response. No persistent server connection or separate publication path is required.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Update latency — weak.** Polling adds an average delay of roughly half the polling interval and can add nearly a full interval, in addition to command and request time. It satisfies the 15-second goal but does not provide prompt push delivery.
+- **Connection and recovery operations — strong.** Stateless requests avoid persistent-connection capacity management and socket reconnect handling. Ordinary HTTPS retries and cursor-based requests provide recovery.
+- **Vendor independence and recurring cost — strong.** Delivery uses infrastructure already operated for HTTPS commands, without a realtime provider or usage-based realtime bill.
+- **Server and browser implementation burden — partial.** Cursor queries and a browser polling loop are straightforward, but every browser repeatedly reads even when no changes occur, increasing storage and request load.
+- **Authoritative recovery — strong.** A cursor query can retrieve missed revisions or establish that the browser needs a fresh snapshot, provided the change store retains the required history.
+
+**Summary.** Polling minimizes connection operations and vendor dependency at the cost of delayed updates and repeated reads.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The MarketBoard service accepts persistent WebSocket connections from browsers. The change store retains replayable event revisions and reliably publishes committed changes to the delivery component. Delivery manages socket sessions and pushes revisions; browsers manage reconnects and catch up from their last observed revision before resuming live updates.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Update latency — strong.** Established connections allow committed changes to be pushed immediately, without waiting for a browser's next request.
+- **Connection and recovery operations — weak.** The team owns connection capacity, socket lifecycles, failed delivery handling, and the operational effects of reconnect bursts. Reliable recovery requires coordination between storage, delivery, and browsers.
+- **Vendor independence and recurring cost — strong.** The transport and subscription behavior remain under MarketBoard's control, with no realtime vendor coupling or recurring provider fee.
+- **Server and browser implementation burden — weak.** The change store needs replay and reliable publication, delivery needs session management, and browsers need reconnect and catch-up logic. These obligations span all three downstream components.
+- **Authoritative recovery — strong.** Revision-based replay and snapshot fallback can restore the authoritative board, while connection state can make stale or disconnected views visible.
+
+**Summary.** Self-hosted WebSockets exchange additional implementation and operations work for prompt updates and delivery independence.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** MarketBoard publishes committed changes to a managed realtime provider, and browsers subscribe through the provider's client library. The change store reconciles publication with the provider so that a committed change is not silently omitted. The delivery component wraps provider integration and credentials; browsers follow the provider's subscription and recovery model.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Update latency — strong.** The provider can push committed changes to subscribed browsers without polling delays.
+- **Connection and recovery operations — strong.** The provider operates most socket capacity and connection infrastructure, although MarketBoard still owns integration failures, credentials, browser recovery, and publication reconciliation.
+- **Vendor independence and recurring cost — weak.** Delivery behavior depends on provider APIs and operating limits, and usage introduces recurring fees. Moving providers would require changes to delivery and browser integration.
+- **Server and browser implementation burden — partial.** Managed connections reduce infrastructure work, but publication reconciliation, provider wrappers, credentials, and the provider-specific subscription model remain substantial parts of the design.
+- **Authoritative recovery — partial.** Provider replay can assist recovery, but MarketBoard must ensure that its authoritative revision history and 20-minute recovery guarantee do not depend on unmatched provider retention or delivery semantics.
+
+**Summary.** A hosted service reduces connection operations while retaining fast updates, but introduces recurring cost, provider coupling, and reconciliation work.
+
+## Selection
+
+MarketBoard uses **self-hosted WebSockets**. Their strong fit for update latency and vendor independence outweighs their weak fit for connection operations and implementation burden. Polling remains operationally attractive but sacrifices prompt updates and creates repeated reads; a hosted service offers similar latency with less connection ownership, but its recurring fees and provider coupling are not justified for this board. The decision accepts operational work deliberately rather than treating minimum operational burden as the overriding goal.
+
+This choice relies on the HLD's permission to use either self-hosted or managed infrastructure. It preserves one authoritative event revision per committed edit and requires connectivity failures to remain visible rather than allowing a stale browser view to appear current.
+
+Committing to this option requires three coordinated downstream designs: the change store must provide replayable revisions and reliable publication; delivery must own WebSocket sessions, connection capacity, and publication handoff; browser sync must reconnect, expose connection state, and catch up from revisions. It forecloses treating delivery as stateless polling or delegating subscription behavior to a hosted provider, but it does not require subsecond delivery or multi-region active-active operation.
