 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Committed changes delivered over self-hosted WebSockets.** The system operates its own socket sessions and accepts the associated connection-capacity and reconnect work to provide fast updates without realtime-vendor coupling or recurring vendor fees. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it adds repeated reads and several seconds of lag despite simpler stateless operation. A hosted realtime service was rejected because its lower connection-operations burden was not worth provider coupling and recurring fees. See the [self-hosted WebSockets decision](decisions/self-hosted-websockets.md).
